  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the statements separately.\n\n- Statement I is true. The graph $y={h}^{′}(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at $x=-2$ meaning that ${h}^{′}(x)=0.$ Moreover, ${h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign by going from positive to negative. Thus, there is a relative maximum at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The slope of the tangent line to $y={h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign somewhere near $x=0.$ Hence, the graph $y=h(x)$ has an inflection point inside $(-2,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that ${h}^{′}(3)=0$ but ${h}^{′}(x)$ does not change its sign around this point. Thus, there is no extremum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
